1. Executive Overview
The discussions describe the evolution of AlgoMind from an SMC-style Expert Advisor into a broader, adaptive market-state and order-flow system. The central design question became not simply 'what is the entry signal?' but 'which market hypothesis is currently most appropriate?' The two dominant hypotheses are Mean Reversion and Trend/Continuation. Market structure, liquidity, proxy delta, VWAP, volume profile/value areas, news and options/dealer-positioning context are intended to interact as evidence used to choose between those hypotheses.
The project repeatedly emphasized that retail MT5 execution and data are materially different from institutional centralized order-flow environments. Consequently, the system must distinguish true exchange order flow from broker-derived proxies, normalize broker symbol specifications, model execution costs, and abstain when data quality is inadequate.
Another major theme is validation integrity. A previous M5 validation review found FVG feature-construction defects that could introduce look-ahead bias. The corrected system therefore needs event-time feature construction: every historical feature must represent only information that was available at that exact decision time.
1.1 Core thesis distilled from the discussions
Markets are state-dependent; one strategy should not be forced to trade every regime.
Mean reversion and continuation are complementary hypotheses rather than mutually exclusive indicators.
Order-flow signals are strongest when interpreted at meaningful market locations.
VWAP and value-area information provide context for whether price is stretched, accepted or rejected.
Proxy delta should be treated as evidence with data-source uncertainty, not as universal centralized order flow.
News can change the regime abruptly and therefore needs its own market-state logic.
Options/dealer-positioning information can provide higher-level context, but its usefulness depends heavily on data quality and empirical validation.
ML should initially rank or gate rule-generated setups rather than replace the entire trading logic.
Hard risk, execution and data-quality controls must remain above the ML layer.
The correct output of the system can be 'no trade' when the evidence is contradictory.
