3. SMC / Market-Structure Layer
The SMC layer discussed in the chats is a contextual layer rather than a complete strategy by itself.
Swing structure: higher highs/higher lows and lower highs/lower lows.
Breaks of structure and changes in market character.
Liquidity pools around prior highs/lows, equal highs/lows, session extremes and obvious stop locations.
Order blocks as contextual supply/demand zones.
Fair Value Gaps as imbalance/retracement areas.
Liquidity sweeps as events in which price takes a known high/low before either rejecting or accepting.
The structural event must be timestamped when it became knowable; later confirmation cannot be allowed to rewrite the historical past.
4. Order-Flow Framework
4.1 Cumulative / proxy delta
The discussions use delta as an indication of aggressive buying versus selling. However, for retail spot FX and many CFD feeds, the available data may not represent a centralized market-wide transaction flow. Therefore, the system should explicitly label broker-derived delta as proxy delta.
Order-flow concept
Intended interpretation
Important condition
Delta divergence
Price makes an extreme while delta fails to confirm
Potential exhaustion; not a standalone reversal signal.
Delta flip
Delta changes directional sign
Potential trigger after location/context is established.
Delta surge
Unusually large directional delta movement
Evidence of participation/impulse.
Delta transition
The character of flow changes over a sequence
Useful for detecting regime/participation change.
Absorption/exhaustion
Aggressive flow produces limited price progress
Can support rejection/reversion when located correctly.
Imbalance
Evidence of one-sided participation or thin liquidity
Must be interpreted according to the available feed.

4.2 Order flow must be contextual
A major design conclusion is that the same delta observation can have different meanings depending on where it occurs. For example, aggressive selling near a major value-area low followed by a failure to push lower is potentially different from aggressive selling during a confirmed breakdown with continued acceptance below the level.
Order flow + location is stronger than order flow alone.
Order flow + structure + volatility is stronger than one isolated delta event.
The system should measure normalized delta, such as z-scores, percentiles and delta relative to recent volume.
Data quality should be a feature and a veto condition.
5. VWAP, Volume Profile and Value Area
5.1 VWAP
VWAP provides a volume-weighted reference price for a defined session or anchor.
Distance from VWAP can be normalized by ATR, realized volatility or historical VWAP-deviation distributions.
Above/below VWAP is contextual rather than an automatic buy/sell rule.
Extreme deviation combined with exhaustion can support mean reversion.
Persistent acceptance on one side of VWAP can support continuation.
5.2 Value area
VAH = Value Area High.
VAL = Value Area Low.
POC = Point of Control.
High-volume nodes can represent areas of prior acceptance.
Low-volume nodes can represent areas through which price may move quickly when accepted/rejected.
Inside value, balanced/rotational behavior may be more likely.
A brief excursion beyond VAH/VAL is not automatically a breakout; acceptance beyond the boundary is more important.
Migration of value can indicate that the market is establishing a new accepted price region.
6. The Two Main Strategy Hypotheses
6.1 Hypothesis A — Mean Reversion / Value Reversion
The mean-reversion hypothesis assumes that price has become sufficiently extended from an accepted-value reference and that the extension is losing directional strength. The objective is a rotation back toward VWAP, POC, value midpoint, the opposite value edge or another statistically supported equilibrium.
Component
Possible implementation
Location
Outside value, near VAH/VAL, large VWAP deviation, prior range extreme or liquidity extreme.
Extension
VWAP/value distance standardized by volatility.
Exhaustion
Delta divergence, weakening flow, absorption-like behavior, failed continuation or liquidity sweep.
Trigger
Delta flip, rejection, structure shift or re-entry into value.
Invalidation
Sustained acceptance outside value, continued directional structure or volatility regime change.
Targets
VWAP, POC, value midpoint, opposite value edge or staged targets.
Best environment
Balanced, rotational or exhausted markets.
Danger environment
Strong directional trend days with repeated acceptance away from value.

6.2 Hypothesis B — Trend / Continuation
The continuation hypothesis assumes that the market has escaped a meaningful structure or value boundary and is accepting price in the new area. The system seeks a pullback or continuation entry while directional participation remains intact.
Component
Possible implementation
Location
Break of major structure, liquidity boundary, VAH/VAL, session extreme or other validated level.
Acceptance
Price holds beyond the level rather than immediately returning through it.
Participation
Delta surge/persistence, volume expansion or directional flow.
Entry
Retest, pullback, FVG/OB reaction or continuation trigger.
Invalidation
Failure to hold the breakout, opposing structure shift or strong opposing absorption.
Targets
Next liquidity pool, profile node, session extreme or risk-multiple target.
Best environment
Directional expansion and macro repricing.
Danger environment
Low-volatility chop and false breakouts.

7. How the Components Interact
The intended logic is hierarchical rather than a simple indicator checklist:
Market state → determine whether the market is balanced, trending, expanding, compressing or transitioning.
Location → determine whether price is near value, far from value, at liquidity or beyond a structural boundary.
Structure → determine what price is attempting to do.
Order flow → determine whether participation confirms or contradicts that attempt.
News → determine whether the environment is normal or undergoing event-driven repricing.
Options/dealer context → add higher-level positioning information where reliable data exist.
Strategy selector → estimate whether mean reversion or continuation has the better conditional expectancy.
Risk/execution gate → determine whether the trade can actually be executed safely.
Execution → enter only after all mandatory gates pass.
