17. Machine Learning Layer
The discussions considered whether an AI model is necessary. The recommended direction is to establish a robust rule-based system first, then use ML where it solves a defined problem.
ML role
How it fits
Regime probability
Estimate probability of MR versus continuation conditions.
Trade-quality gate
Score rule-generated candidate setups.
Expected-value model
Estimate outcome conditional on setup/context.
Threshold adaptation
Adjust thresholds within controlled boundaries.
Drift monitoring
Detect changes in feature distributions and performance.

17.1 What ML should not control
It should not override hard risk limits.
It should not bypass spread or execution gates.
It should not be trained using leaked future features.
It should not initially invent arbitrary trades with no interpretable context.
It should not continuously retrain on its own feedback without safeguards.
18. Backtesting and Statistical Validation
18.1 FVG look-ahead defect identified
A previous M5 validation review identified three important problems: nearest_open_fvg was using final-lifecycle/final-invalidated information; _precompute_fvg_dir_flags behaved like once-found sticky-forever flags; and fill_percentage in build_feature_vector used the final maximum fill rather than the fill known at each historical bar.
This is a severe validation issue because it can make historical performance look artificially strong. The fix is to reconstruct the FVG lifecycle at each timestamp and expose only the information available at that timestamp.
18.2 Correct causal design
Record FVG creation time.
Record each subsequent fill update as it occurs.
Record the first actual invalidation time.
At historical bar t, expose only the state known by t.
Never use the final fill percentage for earlier observations.
Never let a once-found flag remain active forever unless the feature is intentionally cumulative.
Unit-test feature vectors bar by bar against a deterministic event timeline.
18.3 Validation protocol
Chronological train/validation/test/forward splits.
Walk-forward evaluation.
No random shuffling for final time-series evaluation.
Realistic spread, commission, slippage and execution delay.
Parameter-sensitivity analysis.
Ablation testing.
Monte Carlo trade-sequence analysis.
Cross-instrument and cross-regime testing.
Out-of-sample forward testing.
Model and feature-version freezing before final evaluation.
