20. Multiple Strategies and Prop-Firm Risk
The discussions also considered whether running multiple strategies on one prop-firm account is mathematically preferable to one strategy. The conclusion was that multiple strategies can improve robustness only if they genuinely diversify regime exposure and their combined risk is controlled.
Two strategies with high return correlation are not true diversification.
Mean reversion and continuation can potentially diversify because their strongest environments differ.
Portfolio-level drawdown must govern the combined strategies.
Each strategy can have a risk budget, but the total account risk must have a hard ceiling.
Do not allow simultaneous signals to multiply effective risk beyond the intended account limit.
21. Hardware and Deployment Constraints
The project was designed with a low-end development machine in mind: a Lenovo IdeaPad-class 10th-generation i3 system with 4 GB RAM and an HDD. This makes computational efficiency important.
Prefer incremental calculations instead of recomputing complete history on every tick.
Keep feature vectors compact.
Use MQL5 for execution-critical lightweight operations.
Avoid unnecessary deep-learning models.
Use bounded caches and controlled logging.
Move heavy analytics to a VPS or remote service when needed.
Design Python services so they can be deployed independently of the local laptop.
22. End-to-End Build Plan
1. Freeze definitions: Write exact mathematical definitions for structure, FVG lifecycle, delta flip/surge/divergence, VWAP deviation, value acceptance, liquidity sweep, trend strength and news states.
2. Build causal feature engine: Ensure every feature can be reconstructed using only information available at the decision timestamp.
3. Build standalone baselines: Create independent MR and continuation strategies before combining them.
4. Add VWAP/value: Measure whether location and acceptance improve the baselines.
5. Add order flow: Add proxy delta and quantify incremental information.
6. Add news: Implement pre-event, reaction, confirmation and stabilization states.
7. Add options context: Use reliable positioning/IV/strike information where available.
8. Build rule-based regime selector: Start transparent and interpretable.
9. Add ML gatekeeper: Train on candidate setups generated by the validated rule engine.
10. Integrate MQL5 execution: Enforce broker specifications, risk, spread, session and news controls.
11. Walk forward: Test chronologically across multiple regimes.
12. Stress test: Apply spread/slippage/latency shocks and Monte Carlo analysis.
13. Forward test: Use live-like data and execution without financial risk.
14. Controlled deployment: Start at low risk with kill switches and monitoring.
